    "description": "The Champion 2500 is the unit we would buy for a typical household, and the reason is that it is small and quiet enough to actually get used rather than dreaded. At under 40 pounds, one person carries it out of the garage without a second set of hands — a detail that sounds trivial until you have wrestled a 120 lb generator down porch steps in the rain. On our mixed household load of refrigerator, lighting, and device charging it measured 55 dB at 23 feet, quiet enough that we ran it overnight twenty feet from a bedroom window without complaint. Output is honest: 2,500 peak and 1,850 running on gasoline, dropping to roughly 2,250/1,665 on propane, which comfortably covered a full-size refrigerator plus lights, a router, phones, and a television at once. It will not touch a well pump or central air, and no amount of optimism changes that. Cold-start behavior was among the best here, firing on the second pull at 34°F on both fuels where two competitors needed choke fiddling. Parallel capability is the feature that makes the sizing decision low-risk: if your needs grow, a second identical unit doubles output for less than the price of stepping up to a 5,000W machine, and you keep the portability of two small units. The fuel gauge is the one genuinely weak detail — it is small and hard to read in the dark, which is exactly when you need it."
