    "description": "We debated including the EU2200i in a dual fuel roundup because Honda does not sell it that way. It earns its place because the aftermarket conversion kits are mature, widely used, and reliable, and because nothing else here matches this engine. Across every test session, including cold starts at 34°F, it fired on the first pull — no other unit in this group managed that consistently. It was also the quietest machine we tested, measuring 52 dB under our mixed household load, genuinely conversation-level and low enough to run near an open bedroom window. Output is modest at 2,200 peak and 1,800 running on gasoline, dropping roughly 10% on propane through a conversion kit, which puts it in the essentials-only category: refrigerator, lights, devices, a television. Build quality is the argument for the price, and it shows in details like the fuel shutoff, the panel layout, and how it feels after five years of use rather than five hours. Resale is a real financial consideration too — used EU2200i units routinely sell for 70% of new price years later, which narrows the effective cost gap against cheaper machines considerably. The honest caveats: the conversion is aftermarket, which typically affects Honda's warranty coverage, and even before that this is the most expensive way to buy 2,200 watts. If reliability is the thing you care most about, it is still the answer."
