        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Dual Fuel Plus Inverter Is the Useful Combination -->
        <div id="why-dual-fuel-inverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Dual Fuel Plus Inverter Is the Useful Combination
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two separate technologies get bundled into this category name, and it is worth separating them because they solve different problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Inverter</strong> describes how the generator makes electricity. A conventional generator's alternator spins at a fixed speed and its output quality depends on that speed staying constant. An inverter generator produces raw AC, rectifies it to DC, then electronically rebuilds a clean sine wave — which means the engine can throttle down when the load is light. That delivers three real benefits: total harmonic distortion typically under 3% (safe for anything with a circuit board), dramatically lower noise at partial load, and much better fuel efficiency because the engine is not running at 3,600 rpm to power a phone charger.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dual fuel</strong> describes what it burns. A dual fuel unit has a selector valve and a propane regulator alongside the normal gasoline carburetor. The practical value is availability rather than economy. In the aftermath of a regional storm, gas stations without power cannot pump, and the ones that can have hour-long lines. Propane exchange cages and 20 lb tanks in garages are unaffected. Having both options is genuine emergency resilience, not a marketing feature.
