          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Strategy That Actually Works</h3>
          <p>
            The owners who get the most out of these machines use both fuels deliberately rather than picking one. Store propane as the strategic reserve — it keeps for a decade, so three or four 20 lb tanks in the garage represent a week of essential power that never goes stale. Use gasoline when you want maximum output or when temperatures are near freezing. And critically, <strong>run the unit on propane for its monthly exercise runs</strong>: propane burns clean and leaves no varnish, so the carburetor stays healthy in a machine that mostly sits.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Cold Weather Limitation Nobody Mentions</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Propane must vaporize inside the tank to feed the engine, and vaporization slows sharply as temperature drops. Below roughly 20°F a 20 lb tank struggles to keep up with demand, and the generator will surge or refuse to hold load — precisely during a winter storm when you need it. Keep gasoline on hand for cold-weather outages, or use a larger 100 lb tank, which has far more surface area for vaporization.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

