        <!-- SECTION 6: Propane Tank Sizing, Storage, and Rotation -->
        <div id="propane-storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Propane Tank Sizing, Storage, and Rotation
          </h2>

          <p>
            The propane side of a dual fuel generator is only as useful as the fuel you actually have on hand when the power goes out. Getting the tank strategy right is what turns a spec-sheet feature into genuine days of runtime.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much Propane to Keep</h3>
          <p>
            A 20-pound tank — the standard barbecue size — holds roughly 4.6 gallons and runs a mid-size inverter generator for about 6 to 9 hours at moderate load. That maths is the whole planning exercise: three tanks is roughly a day of continuous essential power, and four to six tanks covers the two-to-three-day outage that most households actually experience. Because propane does not degrade, tanks bought once sit ready for years without attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            For anyone in an area with genuinely long outages, stepping up to a 100-pound tank changes the equation twice over. It holds roughly five times the fuel of a 20-pound bottle, and — more importantly in winter — its much larger surface area vaporises propane far faster, which is exactly what fails on a small tank in deep cold. The trade is that a full 100-pound tank weighs around 170 pounds and is not something you move casually or refill at an exchange cage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exchange or Refill</h3>
          <p>
            Tank exchange cages are convenient and available almost everywhere, but they typically fill to 15 pounds rather than the full 20, so you are paying for a portion of fuel you do not receive. Refilling at a propane dealer usually costs meaningfully less per gallon and gives you a genuinely full tank. For a household keeping several tanks as emergency reserve, refilling is the better economics; for a single spare, convenience usually wins.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storing Tanks Safely</h3>
          <p>
            Propane tanks belong outdoors or in a detached, ventilated structure — never in a basement, never in living space, and not in an attached garage in most jurisdictions. Store them upright, secured so they cannot tip, out of direct sun, and away from anything that could ignite a leak. Check the valve and O-ring seal each time you connect, and soap-test the connection rather than trusting it by feel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tanks also carry a certification date stamped on the collar and require requalification after twelve years, then every five to ten years after that. An out-of-date tank will be refused at a refill counter, which is an unwelcome discovery the day before a forecast storm. Check the stamps on your reserve tanks once a year rather than at the moment you need them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Safety and Maintenance -->
        <div id="safety-maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety and Maintenance
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Carbon Monoxide Applies to Propane Too</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Propane burns cleaner than gasoline but still produces carbon monoxide, which is odorless and can be fatal within minutes. Never run any fuel-burning generator in a garage, breezeway, covered porch, or within 20 feet of a window or door — including on propane. Several units here include CO shutoff sensors; treat those as a backup, never a permission slip.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exercise It Monthly, on Propane</h3>
          <p>
            The most common failure mode for a stored generator is a gummed carburetor from stale gasoline. Running the unit under load for 20-30 minutes monthly keeps everything moving, and doing those runs on propane means no fuel varnish is deposited at all. This single habit eliminates the majority of no-start failures.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drain or Stabilize Gasoline for Storage</h3>
          <p>
            If you store the unit with gasoline in it, add stabilizer and run the engine long enough to pull treated fuel through the carburetor. If you store it dry, run the carburetor bowl empty by shutting the fuel valve while it runs. Either works; leaving untreated fuel sitting for six months does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil Changes and Break-In</h3>
          <p>
            Most inverter generators want their first oil change after 20-25 hours, then every 50-100 hours. These are small engines with small sumps — a quart or less — so oil degrades faster than in a car and a missed change matters more. Check the level before every extended run, not just at intervals.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inspect the Propane Hose and Regulator</h3>
          <p>
            The LPG hose and regulator are rubber components that age, and a leak at the connection is a fire risk. Check for cracking annually, replace the hose every five years or so, and soap-test connections whenever you attach a tank.
          </p>
        </div>

