              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is propane safer than gasoline for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In storage, yes — propane does not degrade, does not spill, and does not create fire risk from vapors in a garage. During operation, both produce carbon monoxide, which is odorless and can be fatal within minutes. Every fuel-burning generator, propane included, must run outdoors at least 20 feet from windows and doors.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes With Dual Fuel Inverter Generators
          </h2>

          <p>
            These machines are forgiving in use and unforgiving in storage. Most of the failures we hear about are preventable.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing Against the Gasoline Rating While Planning to Run Propane</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The propane derate is about 10%, and it lands exactly where it hurts — on the surge capacity that starts a well pump or refrigerator compressor. If propane is your primary fuel, size against the propane number on the spec sheet, not the headline gasoline figure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Untreated Gasoline in It for Months</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gummed carburetor is the single most common reason a stored generator will not start when needed. Either stabilize the fuel and run it through the carburetor, or shut the fuel valve and let the bowl run dry before storage. Better yet, do your monthly exercise runs on propane so no varnish forms at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Propane Means No Carbon Monoxide</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Propane burns cleaner but still produces carbon monoxide in quantities that kill. The 20-foot setback from windows, doors, and vents applies identically on propane, and a garage with the door open is not outdoors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on Propane Alone in Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Below roughly 20°F a 20 lb propane tank cannot vaporize fast enough to feed the engine at load, and the generator surges or quits. Winter storms are exactly when outages happen, so keep gasoline on hand or use a larger tank with more vaporization surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying One Big Unit When Two Small Ones Fit Better</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two parallel-capable 2,200W units cost about the same as one 4,500W machine, but each is liftable by one person, they can be used separately, and a failure of one still leaves you with power. If you are torn between sizes, buying one now and adding a second later is often the better plan.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

